name: pipes
intro: A pipe is a one-way tube inside the kernel. `pipe(fd)` fills in two
intro: descriptors: `fd[0]` reads, `fd[1]` writes. Bytes put in at one end
intro: come out of the other, in order.
intro:
intro:     int fd[2];
intro:     if (pipe(fd) != 0) { ...fail... }
intro:
intro: On its own that is useless; the trick is `fork`, which hands the
intro: child a copy of both descriptors. Now two processes can talk. The
intro: deal is that each keeps the end it uses and closes the other:
intro:
intro:     child:   close(fd[0]);  write(fd[1], ...);  close(fd[1]);
intro:     parent:  close(fd[1]);  read(fd[0], ...);   close(fd[0]);
intro:
intro: This is the rule that bites everyone. `read` on a pipe returns 0 --
intro: end of file -- only when every copy of the write end is closed, in
intro: every process. A parent that forgets `close(fd[1])` holds a write
intro: end itself, so its own `read` waits forever for bytes it will never
intro: send. Close what you do not use, in every process, always.
intro:
intro: Both calls move fewer bytes than you ask for when they like: a pipe
intro: holds about 64 KB, and `read` gives what is there now. Loop until
intro: you have it all, and keep a growing buffer if you do not know the
intro: size.
intro:
intro: To make a program's stdin or stdout the pipe, use `dup2(old, new)`:
intro: afterwards descriptor `new` is another name for what `old` points
intro: at. 0 is stdin and 1 is stdout (`STDIN_FILENO`, `STDOUT_FILENO`).
intro:
intro:     dup2(fd[1], STDOUT_FILENO);  /* printf now goes into the pipe */
intro:     close(fd[0]);                /* the originals are not needed */
intro:     close(fd[1]);
intro:     execlp("ls", "ls", (char *)NULL);
intro:
intro: `fdopen(fd, "w")` wraps a descriptor in a `FILE *`.
intro:
intro: An exec'd program keeps descriptors 0, 1 and 2, and never knows they
intro: are a pipe. That is all `a | b` is: two forks, one pipe, one `dup2`
intro: on each side, and every spare end closed. Last warning: writing to a
intro: pipe nobody reads kills you with SIGPIPE. The next unit is about
intro: that.
---
q: pipemsg.c -- send bytes through a pipe
spec: Take at most one argument, TEXT (none means empty text). Make a
spec: pipe and fork. The child writes the bytes of TEXT, nothing added,
spec: into the pipe and exits 0. The parent reads from the pipe until end
spec: of file, counts the bytes, waits for the child, and prints
spec: `read N bytes`, then exits 0. TEXT may be much bigger than one
spec: `read` returns, even bigger than the pipe holds.
spec:
spec: Close the ends of the pipe that each process does not use -- if you
spec: forget, the parent never sees end of file and the program hangs.
spec:
spec: More than one argument prints `usage: pipemsg [TEXT]` to standard
spec: error and exits 2. If `pipe` or `fork` fails print
spec: `pipemsg: pipe failed` or `pipemsg: fork failed` to standard error
spec: and exit 1. A child that did not exit 0 prints `pipemsg: child failed`
spec: to standard error and the parent exits 1.
file: pipemsg.c
prog: #define _POSIX_C_SOURCE 200809L
prog: #include <errno.h>
prog: #include <stdio.h>
prog: #include <string.h>
prog: #include <sys/wait.h>
prog: #include <unistd.h>
prog: 
prog: int main(int argc, char **argv)
prog: {
prog: 	if (argc > 2) {
prog: 		fprintf(stderr, "usage: pipemsg [TEXT]\n");
prog: 		return 2;
prog: 	}
prog: 	const char *text = argc == 2 ? argv[1] : "";
prog: 	int fd[2];
prog: 	if (pipe(fd) != 0) {
prog: 		fprintf(stderr, "pipemsg: pipe failed\n");
prog: 		return 1;
prog: 	}
prog: 	pid_t pid = fork();
prog: 	if (pid < 0) {
prog: 		fprintf(stderr, "pipemsg: fork failed\n");
prog: 		return 1;
prog: 	}
prog: 	if (pid == 0) {
prog: 		close(fd[0]);
prog: 		const char *p = text;
prog: 		size_t left = strlen(text);
prog: 		while (left > 0) {
prog: 			ssize_t w = write(fd[1], p, left);
prog: 			if (w < 0) {
prog: 				if (errno == EINTR)
prog: 					continue;
prog: 				_exit(1);
prog: 			}
prog: 			p += w;
prog: 			left -= (size_t)w;
prog: 		}
prog: 		close(fd[1]);
prog: 		_exit(0);
prog: 	}
prog: 	close(fd[1]);
prog: 	char buf[4096];
prog: 	size_t total = 0;
prog: 	for (;;) {
prog: 		ssize_t r = read(fd[0], buf, sizeof buf);
prog: 		if (r < 0 && errno == EINTR)
prog: 			continue;
prog: 		if (r <= 0)
prog: 			break;
prog: 		total += (size_t)r;
prog: 	}
prog: 	close(fd[0]);
prog: 	int status;
prog: 	if (waitpid(pid, &status, 0) < 0 || !WIFEXITED(status) || WEXITSTATUS(status) != 0) {
prog: 		fprintf(stderr, "pipemsg: child failed\n");
prog: 		return 1;
prog: 	}
prog: 	printf("read %zu bytes\n", total);
prog: 	return 0;
prog: }
case: pipemsg hello
case: pipemsg
case: pipemsg "$(head -c 70000 /dev/zero | tr '\0' x)"
case: pipemsg a b; echo $?
hint: child closes fd[0] then loops on write; parent closes fd[1] then loops on read until it returns 0, adding up the counts
